issues.body:
Tested on papaparse 5.7.0, Node 22.

When `unparse()` gets an array of objects, the header is built from the keys of the first object only. Keys that first appear in later rows are dropped from the output without any warning, so data is lost:

```js
const Papa = require('papaparse');

Papa.unparse([{ a: 1, b: 2 }, { a: 3, b: 4, c: 5 }]);
// 'a,b\r\n1,2\r\n3,4'      <- c: 5 is gone
```

The same thing happens with `{ data: [...] }` without `fields`. Rows that are *missing* a key are fine (empty cell), it's only extra keys that vanish. This is easy to hit with data coming from JSON APIs or DB rows where optional fields are only present on some records.

Expected: either the header is the union of keys across all rows, or there's an opt-in for that (e.g. `columns: 'all'`), or at least a documented note that only the first row's keys are used. If you'd go for the union (preserving first-seen order), I can open a PR with tests.

comments.body:
You can export all fields from heterogeneous records using the existing `{ fields, data }` input:

```js
const rows = [{ a: 1, b: 2 }, { a: 3, b: 4, c: 5 }];
const fields = [...new Set(rows.flatMap(Object.keys))];
const data = rows.map(row => fields.map(field => Object.hasOwn(row, field) ? row[field] : undefined));
const csv = fields.length ? Papa.unparse({ fields, data }) : '';
// a,b,c\r\n1,2,\r\n3,4,5
```

Mapping to arrays also keeps an entirely empty record aligned with the header. Papa's [documentation](https://www.papaparse.com/docs#unparse) describes the first-object default and explicit fields/columns; this derives the schema across your full input in first-seen order.

The [copy-in recipe and runnable checks](https://github.com/tevinch/data-shape-kit/tree/main/examples/csv-all-fields) preserve existing export settings and an explicitly selected `columns` list. Nine Node checks pass with Papa Parse 5.7.0, including your example and CSV readback, an empty first record, later fields, multiline values and column restrictions. CSV still represents missing values, nulls and empty strings as empty cells.

issues.body:
I was trying to filter out unwanted columns and rename relevant columns with `transformHeader`, but I could only achieve one or the other.

For example, using the following config, I was able to rename the headers that are in my `headerMap`, but I HAD to return `header` as well. as seen below.

```js
const headerMap = {
  'Code Set': 'codeSet',
  'Code Name': 'codeName',
  'Code Value': 'codeValue',
}

const config = {
    header: true,
    skipEmptyLines: true,
    transformHeader: header => {
      if (header in headerMap) {
        return headerMap[header]
      }
      return header
    }
  }
```

output (as expected):

```
 Data: [
  {
    {codeSet: 'something'},
    {codeName: 'something else'},
    {codeValue: 'more things'},
    {description: 'some description'},
  },
    etc. etc.
]
```

However, when i tried to ignore the non-relevant columns by returning `null` when not found in my header map, the output renamed all headers to `null`. Even when debugging it goes into the positive if block, but still renames everything to null.

```js
const config = {
    header: true,
    skipEmptyLines: true,
    transformHeader: header => {
      if (header in headerMap) {
        return headerMap[header]
      }
      return null
    }
  }
```

output looked something like:

```
 Data: [
  {
    {null: ''},
    {null: ''},
    {null: ''},
    {null: ''},
  },
    etc. etc.
]
```

**Notably**, when using an array of headers instead of a map and not _renaming_ the values, the ignoring with `null` worked correctly.

comments.body:
I'm looking at `transformHeader` issues. Can you explain the last note. I haven't been able to write a test that verifies this behavior. You're saying the following scenario works for you somehow?

```js
{
		description: "transformHeader returning null should filter out columns",
		input: 'A,B,C,D\r\na,b,c,d',
		config: {
			header: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'],
			transformHeader: function(header) {
				if (header === 'A' || header === 'C') {
					return header.toLowerCase();
				}
				return null;
			}
		},
		expected: {
			data: [{"a": "a", "c": "c"}],
			errors: []
		}
},
```

I rechecked the rename-and-filter case with PapaParse 5.7.0. With one unwanted column, `transformHeader` ran once per column, but returning `null` still produced a `"null"` property. So that return value does not remove the column in this case.

One way to handle both operations is to parse with `header: false`, resolve the selected names against the original header row, then construct each object from those column positions. This also keeps values aligned when an unwanted column is in the middle.

I put a small MIT-licensed helper and runnable tests [here](https://github.com/tevinch/data-shape-kit/tree/main/examples/csv-column-map). After copying the two modules and installing `papaparse@5.7.0`, the string-input example is:

```js
import { selectCsvColumns } from './papaparse-columns.mjs';

const rows = selectCsvColumns(
  'Code Set,description,Code Name,Code Value\n001,omit,"Alpha, beta",NA',
  {
    'Code Set': 'codeSet',
    'Code Name': 'codeName',
    'Code Value': 'codeValue',
  },
);
// [{ codeSet: '001', codeName: 'Alpha, beta', codeValue: 'NA' }]
```

Only mapped columns are included. The helper rejects missing or ambiguous selected headers, duplicate output names and rows of the wrong width, and preserves values as strings. This is a separate synchronous string-processing option; it does not change PapaParse's configuration or implement streaming/Worker handling.

For the Node UTF-8 boundary case, decoding in the source is a small application-side adjustment:

```js
import { createReadStream } from 'node:fs';

const input = createReadStream(filePath, { encoding: 'utf8' });
// Pass input to your existing PapaParse Node integration.
```

With PapaParse 5.7.0 and an independent 80-record fixture containing Chinese text, emoji, quoted CRLF and trailing empty fields, one-byte raw Buffer chunks corrupted the text. The same fixture matched every expected record after Node UTF-8 decoding, in both callback/pause-resume and `NODE_STREAM_INPUT` consumption. This checks the Unicode boundary case; the backpressure and other fixes still need their own verification. Node's ordinary decoder can replace malformed UTF-8, so this is not strict input validation.

For applications that also need a strict file-processing contract, I put together a free [MIT streaming example](https://github.com/tevinch/data-shape-kit/tree/main/examples/streaming-csv) using csv-parse and Node pipeline. It awaits each row callback, rejects invalid UTF-8 and duplicate headers, and propagates cancellation and input/callback errors. Its tests use actual streams, including a blocked consumer. It is a separate Node solution with explicit format limits, not a PapaParse patch. The existing #1133–#1136 contributions remain the upstream work.
